    <!-- Home: Sadhana Marg — short teachings from the tradition, one shown at a time.
         Written as what the tradition says and what sadhaks report, never as fact. -->
    <string name="home_wisdom_title">Sadhana Marg</string>
    <string name="home_wisdom_subtitle">Small things sadhaks learn on the path, one at a time.</string>
    <string name="home_wisdom_next">Next teaching</string>

    <string name="wisdom_nadi_shuddhi_title">Nadi Shuddhi — the channels being cleaned</string>
    <string name="wisdom_nadi_shuddhi_body">When japa becomes steady and the swaras sit right, sadhaks describe small twitches in different parts of the body. The tradition calls this a sign of nadi shuddhi — the subtle channels being cleaned. It is said to come only after a very long time of unbroken japa, so do not go looking for it. Keep reciting patiently; this level takes years to reach.</string>

    <string name="wisdom_akshara_title">Akshara — the syllables you are counting</string>
    <string name="wisdom_akshara_body">The Gayatri proper holds 24 aksharas, or syllables, across its three padas. Sanskrit mantras are measured in aksharas and not in words or minutes, because each syllable carries its own sound and weight. This is also what a Purashcharana is measured against: one lakh japa for every akshara the mantra holds. Sound each one fully and the count takes care of itself.</string>

    <string name="wisdom_swara_title">Swara — the pitch that carries the mantra</string>
    <string name="wisdom_swara_body">A Vedic mantra is not only sounds but pitches. Every akshara carries one of three notes — udatta, the raised note; anudatta, the lowered note; and svarita, the fall from raised to low — and that movement is part of the mantra itself. Recited flat, the words are right but something is missing. Turn on Advanced (Vedic) mode in Settings and Anushtanam will assess your swara alongside your pronunciation.</string>

    <string name="wisdom_trikala_title">Trikala Sandhya — the three junctures</string>
    <string name="wisdom_trikala_body">Sandhya means a joining: dawn, midday and dusk, where one part of the day meets the next. The tradition asks the sadhak to sit at these three junctures rather than whenever it happens to be convenient. Keeping even one juncture every single day builds more than one long sitting a week. Anushtanam works out the three timings from the sun where you live.</string>

    <string name="wisdom_mala_title">Mala — counting on 108 beads</string>
    <string name="wisdom_mala_body">A mala carries 108 beads so that the hand keeps the count and the mind is left free for the mantra. Sadhaks count because a sankalpa needs an honest number, not because the number is the point. A small round kept every day goes further than a thousand in one burst and then a week of nothing. Here, Anushtanam is your mala — it counts only what it actually hears.</string>

    <string name="wisdom_ajapa_title">Ajapa — when japa continues on its own</string>
    <string name="wisdom_ajapa_body">Sadhaks describe a stage where the mantra keeps sounding inside without being started — while walking, while working, while falling asleep. The tradition calls this ajapa japa, japa without reciting. It is said to arrive on its own, far along the path, and never by being forced. Until then the work stays simple: sit, recite, count.</string>

    <string name="wisdom_niyama_title">Niyama — the small rules that hold a sadhana</string>
    <string name="wisdom_niyama_body">Sit at the same time, on the same seat, facing the same direction. Come with a clean body and an unhurried mind, and do not announce your count to others. These niyamas, the small rules of the vow, look trivial on any single day. Over years they are what keeps a sadhana from quietly falling away.</string>

    <string name="wisdom_shraddha_title">Shraddha — earnestness before speed</string>
    <string name="wisdom_shraddha_body">Shraddha is faith and earnestness: meaning what you say while you are saying it. Rushing the aksharas to push a number up empties the japa of the very thing that makes it work. Ten japa recited with attention are worth more than a hundred hurried ones. Anushtanam will count either, but only one of them is sadhana.</string>

